WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:02.040
To reason about RAM-backed booting,

00:00:02.260 --> 00:00:06.260
it helps to understand the normal Linux boot path. At a practical level,

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:08.240
firmware starts the machine.

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:13.740
On modern systems this is often UEFI, though older systems may use BIOS-style boot.

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:18.680
Firmware initializes enough hardware to find a boot target and hand control to a

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:19.780
bootloader.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:24.720
The bootloader, such as GRUB, systemd-boot, or another distribution-specific loader,

00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:27.740
presents entries and loads the Linux kernel,

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:31.480
usually with a kernel command line and an initramfs image.

00:00:31.700 --> 00:00:36.980
The kernel starts next. It initializes hardware, memory management, device drivers,

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:39.040
and the early environment it needs.

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:42.380
The initramfs is then used as early user space.

00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:48.280
It can load modules, unlock encrypted devices, assemble storage, discover root devices,

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:52.100
prepare live-media layers, or run distribution-specific scripts.

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:54.800
Eventually the system finds, mounts,

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:59.820
or constructs the real root filesystem and switches from early user space into the normal

00:01:00.040 --> 00:01:01.320
operating system.

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:04.980
RAM-boot designs usually affect this early path.

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:10.240
A live system that supports a toram option may use initramfs logic to copy the compressed

00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:12.460
live image into memory.

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:17.480
An overlay design may have early boot scripts that mount a read-only lower layer and a

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:21.220
writable tmpfs upper layer before presenting a merged root.

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:26.080
A volatile root design may change what is mounted as persistent storage and what is

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:30.940
recreated on each boot. This is why exact commands vary so much.

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:35.480
The safe method depends on the distribution, bootloader, initramfs framework,

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:41.540
filesystem layout, encrypted disk setup, secure boot configuration, hardware drivers,

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:43.280
and storage naming.

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:47.660
A kernel parameter that works on one live distribution may mean nothing on another.

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:52.520
A bootloader edit that is harmless on a virtual machine can make a laptop hard to recover

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:55.700
if there is no known-good entry or recovery media.

00:01:55.920 --> 00:02:00.800
The lesson is conceptual first. Know where the bootloader, kernel, initramfs,

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:03.500
root filesystem, and user space fit.

00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:07.080
Then test changes in a virtual machine, spare disk,

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:09.860
or lab system before touching a primary machine.
